<%# keep in sync with docs/DESIGN.md §1.1–1.3 %>
<% content_for :title, "How it works" %>
<% describe_page "The substrate, the ten-instruction language and the observables of " \
                 "simulator-life.com: a program soup with no fitness function and no " \
                 "reproduction rule." %>

<div class="container-narrow">
  <h1>How it works</h1>
  <p class="text-muted">
    Everything on this site rests on one substrate, one small language and a handful of
    numbers. This page defines them, so a visitor watching the viewer knows what a tape,
    an epoch and a compression ratio are.
  </p>

  <h2>The world</h2>
  <p>
    The world is a 2D torus of <span class="mono">width × height</span> cells. Every cell
    holds one <strong>tape</strong> of <span class="mono">tape_len</span> bytes, 64 by
    default, and tapes are the only state there is.
  </p>
  <p>
    An <strong>epoch</strong> is one interaction per cell, cells visited in a random
    order. A cell picks a random neighbour within <span class="mono">radius</span> (a
    Moore neighbourhood, 1 by default); the two tapes are concatenated,
    <span class="mono">A ++ B</span>, 2×<span class="mono">tape_len</span> bytes; the
    concatenation is executed as a program for at most
    <span class="mono">max_steps</span> instructions (2<sup>13</sup> by default); the
    result is split back into the two cells. Execution mutates the tapes in place: the
    program <em>is</em> the data.
  </p>
  <p>
    There is no fitness function and no reproduction rule. Any copying that happens is
    done by the programs themselves. Energy is optional and off by default: set
    <span class="mono">energy_per_epoch</span> and every cell gets that many instructions
    to spend each epoch, an interaction halts once its two cells have spent theirs, and
    the budget refills at the start of the next epoch. After each epoch every byte is replaced by
    a uniformly random one with probability <span class="mono">mutation_rate</span>. The
    world is uniform by default, but it need not be: set
    <span class="mono">structure</span> to a gradient or a patchwork and that probability
    rises and falls with where a cell sits, so some cells mutate faster than others. Tapes
    are a fixed length by default, but they need not be: set
    <span class="mono">max_tape_len</span> above the length a tape starts at and a head
    that steps off the end of an interaction claims a fresh byte instead of wrapping, so a
    tape lengthens through the programs' own copying. A
    run is fully determined by its parameters and its seed. The substrate is the "BFF" soup
    of Agüera y Arcas et al.,
    <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.19108">"Computational Life"</a> (2024), made
    spatial so that it looks and behaves like a cellular automaton.
  </p>

  <h2>The instruction set</h2>
  <p>
    Ten byte values are instructions; every other byte is a no-op. Both heads start at 0,
    the instruction pointer starts at 0, and execution stops at the end of the tape, at
    <span class="mono">max_steps</span>, or on an unmatched bracket. The heads wrap modulo
    the current length of the concatenation. An eleventh byte,
    <span class="mono">$</span>, is an instruction only for a run that switched it on: it
    steals energy from the partner cell rather than moving a byte, and is a no-op like any
    other byte in every run that has not.
  </p>
  <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="The instruction set">
    <table class="data-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Byte</th>
          <th>Meaning</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="mono">&lt;</td><td>move head0 −1</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="mono">&gt;</td><td>move head0 +1</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="mono">{</td><td>move head1 −1</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="mono">}</td><td>move head1 +1</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="mono">+</td><td>increment the byte at head0</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="mono">-</td><td>decrement the byte at head0</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="mono">.</td><td>copy the byte at head0 → head1</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="mono">,</td><td>copy the byte at head1 → head0</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="mono">[</td><td>jump forward past the matching <span class="mono">]</span> if the byte at head0 is 0</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="mono">]</td><td>jump back to the matching <span class="mono">[</span> if the byte at head0 is non-zero</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="mono">$</td><td>take <span class="mono">steal_amount</span> of energy from the partner cell, less the <span class="mono">steal_loss</span> share destroyed in transit — a no-op unless the run set <span class="mono">steal_amount</span></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>What we measure</h2>
  <p>
    Every <span class="mono">sample_every</span> epochs a run records these observables.
    The engine computes all of them: nothing on this site re-derives a metric.
  </p>
  <dl class="facts facts-prose">
    <dt class="mono">compress_ratio</dt>
    <dd>
      <span class="mono">zlib(all tapes).len / raw.len</span>. A random soup sits near 1.0;
      a soup of replicators collapses well below 0.5. This is the headline signal.
    </dd>

    <dt class="mono">distinct_tapes</dt>
    <dd>How many distinct tape values the world holds.</dd>

    <dt class="mono">top_share</dt>
    <dd>The fraction of cells holding the single most common tape.</dd>

    <dt class="mono">op_density</dt>
    <dd>
      The fraction of bytes that are one of the ten instructions — about 10/256 in a
      random soup.
    </dd>

    <dt class="mono">replicator_count</dt>
    <dd>How many cells pass the replicator test below, on the first of its draws.</dd>

    <dt class="mono">replicator_pass_rate</dt>
    <dd>
      The share of the census's eight independent draws in which some tape passed the
      test: one draw is a coin toss on a tape at the edge of it.
    </dd>

    <dt class="mono">replicator_count_mean</dt>
    <dd>The mean of what those eight draws counted.</dd>

    <dt class="mono" id="replicator-share">replicator_share</dt>
    <dd>
      The share of 256 cells, drawn at random, whose tape reproduces itself in either
      orientation: it runs five generations down a chain against fresh noise, and the copy
      four generations on must agree with the original on three quarters of its bytes, by
      majority over five chains. The census above asks for a same-orientation copy of one
      of the sixteen commonest tapes, and the replicators that dominate every emerged world
      copy themselves in reverse, so the census reads them as nothing. This reading exists
      beside it rather than replacing it. <span class="mono">replicator_share_rotated</span>
      also accepts a copy that lands a few bytes round, and
      <span class="mono">dominant_self_replicates</span> says whether the dominant tape
      passes. Null on the Life substrate and on every sample recorded before they existed.
    </dd>

    <dt class="mono">entropy_bits</dt>
    <dd>The Shannon entropy of the byte distribution.</dd>

    <dt class="mono">alphabet_size</dt>
    <dd>
      How many of the 256 byte values the world still holds. With no mutation only
      <span class="mono">+</span> and <span class="mono">-</span> can mint a byte value,
      so an alphabet can only shrink — and a world down to a couple of letters reads
      compressible without replicating anything.
    </dd>

    <dt class="mono">copy_rate</dt>
    <dd>
      The share of the sampled epoch's interactions that ended with one tape copied
      byte-exactly over the other half, among the pairs whose halves started out
      different. It catches replication in situ, including replicators that only copy with
      a particular partner.
    </dd>

    <dt class="mono">reverse_copy_rate</dt>
    <dd>
      The same count with the copy reversed: one half ends holding the other's tape last
      byte first. A palindrome's copy counts in both rates.
    </dd>

    <dt class="mono">distinct_lineages</dt>
    <dd>
      How many lineages the world holds. Every cell starts its own; after an interaction a
      cell takes its partner's lineage when the tape it ends with differs in fewer bytes
      from the tape its partner arrived with than from the tape it arrived with itself, and
      keeps its own on a tie. It counts descent rather than shape, so two lineages that
      drifted onto the same tape still read as two.
    </dd>

    <dt class="mono">top_lineage_share</dt>
    <dd>The fraction of cells held by the largest lineage.</dd>

    <dt class="mono">lineage_variation</dt>
    <dd>
      Whether a colony is a clone or a drifting population, in bytes per cell. For each of
      the eight largest lineages that hold more than one cell, the tape most of its cells
      hold is its modal tape; the reading is the mean number of bytes by which a cell
      differs from the modal tape of its own lineage, pooled over the cells of those
      lineages. Lineages of a single cell are left out — a cell is a clone of itself, and
      the crowd of them a young soup carries would read as no variation at all. It reads 0
      for a lineage of identical tapes and climbs as mutation spreads one over neighbouring
      tapes.
    </dd>

    <dt class="mono">lineage_variation_oriented</dt>
    <dd>
      The same reading after each cell's tape is put the way round — as it is, or last byte
      first — that sits closer to its lineage's modal tape, keeping it as it is on a tie. The
      replicators of every emerged world copy themselves in reverse, so a lineage of
      near-clones is half one tape and half its reverse, and the aligned reading counts
      every reversed cell as most of a tape of variation. A tape that grew is reversed over
      the bytes it holds, not its whole slot. Lineages themselves are still inherited
      aligned, so after a takeover one lineage can hold many unrelated tapes, and neither
      reading can see past that. Null on every sample recorded before it existed.
    </dd>

    <dt class="mono">copy_cost</dt>
    <dd>
      What one copy costs the dominant replicator, in interpreter steps. The replicator
      test already runs four trials per tested tape; this is the median of the trials that
      copied, read off the most populous tape that passes the test. Null when no tested
      tape replicates. It is the observable a world that got better at copying itself would
      move: the hand-written replicator the engine tests itself against needs 1 794 steps.
    </dd>

    <dt class="mono">copy_latency</dt>
    <dd>
      How soon the dominant tape has written itself, in interpreter steps: the step at which
      the partner half first holds a byte-exact copy of the tape, in either orientation,
      whatever the program goes on to do. <span class="mono">copy_cost</span> counts the
      steps of a copy that halted, and the reverse copiers that dominate every emerged world
      never halt — their loop runs out the step budget — so it reads nothing there. This is
      the median of five trials against fresh random partners, null when fewer than half of
      them complete a copy. <span class="mono">copy_latency_orientation</span> says which
      way round that copy lies: forward, reverse, or both for a palindrome. The hand-written
      replicator reads 1 790 steps, forward; a 64-byte reverse copier that copies a byte
      every four steps reads 255, reverse. Null on the Life substrate and on every sample
      recorded before they existed.
    </dd>

    <dt class="mono">dominant_compressed_len</dt>
    <dd>
      How much tape the dominant tape is, in bytes, once zlib has squeezed it — the
      same compressor the compression ratio uses, so a tape and the world it sits in are
      read on one scale. Beside it <span class="mono">dominant_instruction_count</span>
      counts how many of that tape's bytes the run's instruction set executes. They are the
      baseline for open-endedness: a world whose dominant tape keeps getting more
      complicated after emergence carries more instructions, one that found a recipe and
      stopped reads a flat count. The compressed length is read against the tape's own
      length and never alone — zlib wraps an incompressible tape in 11 bytes, so a tape of
      junk reads its cap plus 11 whatever the cap is. The hand-written replicator reads 36
      bytes and 15 instructions.
      The tape read is the most populous one that passes the replicator test, and the most
      populous tape of the world when none does — after a crossing the copy rate confirmed,
      the tape most cells hold is the thing that is copying. Null only on the Life
      substrate, which has no tapes.
    </dd>

    <dt class="mono">dominant_raw_len</dt>
    <dd>
      The same tape's own length in bytes, before zlib. It is what makes the compressed
      reading readable: compressed over raw near 1 is an incompressible tape — junk — and
      well under 1 is a tape with structure in it. Beside it
      <span class="mono">dominant_tape_hash</span> is a digest of those very bytes (FNV-1a
      64, sixteen hex digits), an identity rather than a magnitude: two consecutive samples
      with different hashes are two different dominant tapes. Null on the Life substrate,
      and on every sample recorded before they existed.
    </dd>

    <dt class="mono">dominant_replicates</dt>
    <dd>
      Whether the tape those two readings describe passed the replicator test. True and they
      are the complexity of a confirmed copier, the very tape
      <span class="mono">copy_cost</span> is priced on; false and they are the complexity of
      the tape the most cells hold, whatever it does.
    </dd>

    <dt class="mono">conserved_core_bytes</dt>
    <dd>
      How many byte positions the largest lineage holds invariant across its members: the
      positions at least nine of every ten of them give one and the same value, read off the
      largest lineage that holds more than one cell. Beside it
      <span class="mono">conserved_core_ops</span> counts how many of those positions hold a
      byte the run's instruction set executes.
      <span class="mono">lineage_variation</span> says how far a lineage has spread; these
      say whether a working core survived the spreading — a conserved copy loop with junk
      around it reads a small core of instructions inside a wide cloud, and turnover at a
      flat tape size reads none at all. A member too short to reach a position holds nothing
      there and agrees with nobody. A lineage of identical tapes reads its whole tape length.
      Null where no lineage holds two cells, on the Life substrate, and on every sample
      recorded before they existed.
    </dd>

    <dt class="mono">conserved_core_bytes_oriented</dt>
    <dd>
      The same core, read after each member of the lineage is put the way round that sits
      closer to its modal tape, as <span class="mono">lineage_variation_oriented</span> puts
      them; <span class="mono">conserved_core_ops_oriented</span> counts its instructions. A
      lineage of a tape and its reverse agrees on almost no position aligned, and on its
      whole length oriented.
    </dd>

    <dt class="mono">lineage_compressed_len</dt>
    <dd>
      The same two readings as <span class="mono">dominant_compressed_len</span>, taken of a
      tape chosen by descent rather than by population: the modal tape of the largest
      lineage, with <span class="mono">lineage_instruction_count</span> beside it. The
      dominant tape is whichever tape most cells hold at that sample, and it rotates — so a
      lineage that keeps getting more complicated while its modal tape turns over reads flat
      through the dominant series and moves through this one. Null where no lineage holds
      two cells, on the Life substrate, and on every sample recorded before they existed.
    </dd>

    <dt class="mono">steal_rate</dt>
    <dd>
      The share of the sampled epoch's interactions in which a steal executed. The steal op
      is off by default: set <span class="mono">steal_amount</span> on a world whose cells
      hold an energy stock and the byte <span class="mono">$</span> becomes an instruction
      that takes that much energy out of the partner cell's stock, destroying the
      <span class="mono">steal_loss</span> share of it on the way. The rate reads 0 wherever
      the op is off, so an arm where theft never evolved can be told from one where it was
      never possible.
    </dd>

    <dt class="mono">transition_epoch</dt>
    <dd>
      Recorded once per run: the first sampled epoch at which
      <span class="mono">compress_ratio</span> drops below 0.6 and stays below it for the
      next 3 samples, counting only samples whose alphabet is alive —
      <span class="mono">op_density</span> at most 0.9 and
      <span class="mono">alphabet_size</span> at least 16. Null until it happens. It is the primary dependent variable of every
      sweep. A flagged run is the signal we sweep on, not a proof of self-replication: the
      replicator census can still read zero, and
      <%= link_to "the findings", findings_path %> say where the two disagree.
    </dd>
  </dl>
  <p>
    <strong>The replicator test</strong>: a tape <span class="mono">T</span> is a
    replicator if executing <span class="mono">T ++ R</span> for a fresh random tape
    <span class="mono">R</span> yields <span class="mono">T</span> in the second half in at
    least 3 of 4 trials. It runs on the <span class="mono">top_k</span> most common tapes
    of each sample, 16 by default, and
    <span class="mono">replicator_count</span> counts the cells holding a tape that passed.
  </p>

  <h2 id="glossary">Words this site uses precisely</h2>
  <dl class="facts facts-prose">
    <dt>transition</dt>
    <dd>
      The detector's event: <span class="mono">compress_ratio</span> under 0.6, held for 3
      samples. It is a property of the compression series, nothing more.
    </dd>

    <dt>flagged</dt>
    <dd>Said of a run whose transition the detector recorded.</dd>

    <dt>replicator</dt>
    <dd>A tape that passes the <span class="mono">T ++ R</span> test above.</dd>

    <dt>census</dt>
    <dd>
      <span class="mono">replicator_count</span>: how many cells hold a passing tape at one
      sample.
    </dd>

    <dt>emergence</dt>
    <dd>
      A transition <em>and</em> a non-zero census in the same run. That is this site's
      reading, stated here because the design of the instrument does not define the word.
    </dd>

    <dt>arm</dt>
    <dd>One grid value of a sweep, with its seeds.</dd>
  </dl>

  <h2>The programme</h2>
  <p>
    Each sweep varies one property of the substrate and asks how the time to emergence
    responds. They run in this order in <%= link_to "the experiments", experiments_path %>.
  </p>
  <ol>
    <% @show.sweeps.each do |sweep| %>
      <li>
        <%= sweep_link(sweep) %> — <%= sweep.description %>
      </li>
    <% end %>
  </ol>
  <p>
    None of them reads as a negative result on its own, which is what the control sweep
    ahead of them all is for —
    <%= sweep_link(@show.control) %>: <%= @show.control.description %>
  </p>
  <% @show.follow_ups.each do |sweep| %>
    <p>
      A sweep that answers nothing can still be re-run at a longer budget, which is what
      <%= sweep_link(sweep) %> does: <%= sweep.description %>
    </p>
  <% end %>
  <p>
    What each of them has found so far, and what is still open, is written up in
    <%= link_to "the findings", findings_path %>.
  </p>
</div>
